17.3  Swap and swappiness

TL;DR
  - Swap is disk space the kernel uses as overflow for RAM: app memory that is
    rarely touched moves there.
  - A little swap in use is fine. Constant swapping in and out is what makes a
    machine slow.
  - zram is swap in compressed RAM: no disk involved. Fedora uses it by default.

      swapon --show
      vmstat 1        # si and so: swapped in and out, per second

On your machine
  RAM       10.5 GiB available of 15.5 GiB (68%) · 1.1 GiB completely free
  Cache     7.5 GiB in buff/cache, 604.0 MiB of it shared (tmpfs)
  Swap      256.0 MiB used of 8.0 GiB · swappiness 60

Pitfalls
  - No swap at all doesn't make a machine faster: the kernel can then only drop
    file cache, and the OOM killer comes sooner.
  - Hibernation writes the RAM to swap: it needs a swap partition or file large
    enough for the RAM in use. zram can't be used for it.
  - A swap file on btrfs needs special settings (no copy-on-write, no
    compression): create it with btrfs filesystem mkswapfile.
  - swapoff -a on a busy machine pulls everything back into RAM, and can wake
    the OOM killer.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 17.3 --why

Related
  Commands  swapon, vmstat
  Paths     /proc/swaps, /proc/sys/vm/swappiness
  Hub       idk memory Memory
  See also  idk 17.1 free vs available
  See also  idk 17.2 The OOM killer
